AB  - Cholangiocarcinoma has historically represented a major contraindication to liver 
      transplantation at many centers because of its high recurrence rate and low 
      disease-free survival rate, even after radical surgery. Novel neoadjuvant therapy 
      protocols combined with demolitive surgery and liver transplantation seem to 
      achieve successful results in terms of overall and disease-free survivals. 
      Surgery frequently seems to be unsatisfactory only for patients also suffering 
      from chronic cirrhosis or end-stage liver disease. We have reported a case of 
      hilar cholangiocarcinoma occurring in a case of primary sclerosing cholangitis 
      treated with neoadjuvant radiochemotherapy and endoscopic brachytherapy, followed 
      by liver transplantation combined with pancreatoduodenectomy, who has survived 
      free of disease for >8 years.
