PG  - 328-35
AB  - Due to their anatomical position, the tendency of early infiltrative growth and 
      their poor prognosis without treatment, klatskin tumors are challenging 
      concerning diagnosis and therapy. In contrast to other tumors of the 
      gastrointestinal tract, for which exact diagnostic and stage dependent 
      therapeutic guidelines could be formulated, clear recommendations for klatskin 
      tumors are missing. Thus, survival rates after local resection, e. g. resection 
      of the bile duct bifurcation alone, show high rates of R1/2 resection and early 
      tumor recurrence. With an additional hepatic resection formally curative 
      resections and long-term survival can be improved. Extended liver resections 
      including the portal vein provide the highest rates of R0 resections for hilar 
      carcinomas of the extrahepatic bile duct. Survival rates after liver 
      transplantation for klatskin tumors are not yet convincing. Promising first 
      results have been reported for the combination of neoadjuvant treatment and liver 
      transplantation and might show future perspectives for the treatment of klatskin 
      tumors.
